## Why This Is Asked
Prompts candidates to design CKNE-driven admission control and fairness in real-time multi-tenant streams, a practical production concern.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE signals to compute per-tenant health
- Dynamic rate limiting and cross-tenant shedding
- Payload schema for health metadata and token updates
- Minute-level control loop with smoothing

## Code Example

```javascript
function healthScore(p95Latency, errRate, queueDepth){
  const normLat = Math.min(p95Latency / 1000, 1);
  const normErr = Math.min(errRate, 1);
  const normQ = Math.min(queueDepth / 1000, 1);
  // Weighted mix: latency matters more
  return 0.55 * normLat + 0.25 * normErr + 0.20 * normQ;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test tail latency and fairness under correlated bursts?
- How would you handle tenants with bursty, short-lived traffic without starving others?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests applying CKNE to practical throughput controls using simple token budgets; ties beginner design to real-world load management.

## Key Concepts
- CKNE to per-tenant rate and queue depth mapping
- Minute-loop throttling and token rebalancing
- Delaying non-critical logs while preserving SLA baseline
- Observability and a simple test plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// CKNE to tokens mapping (simplified)
function tokensForCKNE(ckne, maxRate){
  return Math.max(1, Math.floor(ckne * maxRate));
}
function shouldDrop(log, ckne, queueDepth){
  const threshold = 100 * (1 - ckne);
  return queueDepth > threshold && log.priority !== 'critical';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test with fluctuating CKNE values?
- How would you extend to different log severities and multi-tenant fairness?
